The CSS box model describes how a browser calculates the space occupied by an HTML element. Every element consists of four layers: **content, padding, border, and margin**. Content is the actual text or image inside the element. Padding creates space between the content and the border. The border surrounds the content and padding, while the margin creates space outside the border, separating the element from others. By default, the `content-box` model adds padding and borders to the specified width and height. Using `box-sizing: border-box` includes padding and borders within the declared dimensions, making layouts easier to manage.
